A maintainer on the project lfrmonteiro99/memento-mcp (TypeScript) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #27: feat(graph): typed-edge knowledge graph over memories (v1: schema + 4 MCP tools)

## Context

Follow-up to #7 (closed as `not_planned`). The original spec was comprehensive but bundled too much into one PR (schema + 4 tools + auto-link inline tags + ranker boost + web inspector integration). This issue ships the **MVP wedge**: the data model and the four MCP tools that operate on it. Optional integrations are deferred to separate follow-ups so each can land in a small, reviewable PR.

The single relationship memento-mcp captures today is `memories.supersedes_memory_id`. Real codebase knowledge has more structure: a *pitfall* is **caused-by** a *pattern*, an *architecture* note **references** a *decision*. Without explicit edges, search can't follow context and the LLM re-derives structure from raw text every turn. Adding a typed-edge layer turns memento-mcp from "tagged document store" into "knowledge graph over typed memories" — the single biggest differentiator we can ship.

## Goal

Add a `memory_edges` table and **four** new MCP tools:

- `memory_link(from_id, to_id, edge_type, weight?)` — create an edge
- `memory_unlink(from_id, to_id, edge_type)` — remove
- `memory_graph(id, depth=2, edge_types?, direction?)` — return local subgraph as adjacency list
- `memory_path(from_id, to_id, max_hops=4)` — shortest path between two memories

Six edge types ship in v1: `relates_to`, `supersedes`, `caused_by`, `mitigated_by`, `references`, `implements`. The set is closed (Zod enum) so queries stay predictable.

## Design

### Schema — migration v8 in `src/db/database.ts`

Follow the existing migration pattern (v6 `privacy_private_tags`, v7 `sync_state`). Append a new entry to the `migrations` array with `version: 8`:

```sql
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS memory_edges (
  from_id    TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES memories(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
  to_id      TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES memories(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
  edge_type  TEXT NOT NULL,
  weight     REAL NOT NULL DEFAULT 1.0,
  created_at TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now')),
  PRIMARY KEY (from_id, to_id, edge_type)
);
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_memory_edges_from ON memory_edges(from_id);
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_memory_edges_to   ON memory_edges(to_id);
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_memory_edges_type ON memory_edges(edge_type);
```

Backfill existing supersedes data atomically as part of the migration:

```sql
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO memory_edges (from_id, to_id, edge_type, weight, created_at)
SELECT id, supersedes_memory_id, 'supersedes', 1.0, created_at
FROM memories
WHERE supersedes_memory_id IS NOT NULL;
```

The `memories.supersedes_memory_id` column stays for backwards compat. Going forward, `MemoriesRepo.store()` writes both the column AND the edge row in the same transaction.

### Repo — new module `src/db/edges.ts`

```ts
export type EdgeType =
  | "relates_to" | "supersedes" | "caused_by"
  | "mitigated_by" | "references" | "implements";

export const ALLOWED_EDGE_TYPES: ReadonlyArray<EdgeType> = [
  "relates_to", "supersedes", "caused_by",
  "mitigated_by", "references", "implements",
];

export interface EdgeRow {
  from_id: string;
  to_id: string;
  edge_type: EdgeType;
  weight: number;
  created_at: string;
}

export class EdgesRepo {
  constructor(private db: Database.Database) {}
  link(fromId: string, toId: string, type: EdgeType, weight?: number): void;
  unlink(fromId: string, toId: string, type: EdgeType): boolean;
  outgoing(id: string, types?: EdgeType[]): EdgeRow[];
  incoming(id: string, types?: EdgeType[]): EdgeRow[];
  subgraph(rootId: string, depth: number, types?: EdgeType[], direction?: "out"|"in"|"both"): { nodes: string[]; edges: EdgeRow[] };
  shortestPath(fromId: string, toId: string, maxHops: number, types?: EdgeType[]): string[] | null;
}
```

`link()` rejects self-edges (`fromId === toId`) and unknown edge types with a clear error. Uses `INSERT OR REPLACE` so re-linking with a different weight is idempotent.

`subgraph` and `shortestPath` are BFS over `outgoing`+`incoming`. Visited set prevents cycles. Return adjacency

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Memory knowledge graph: typed-edge layer over memories with BFS walkers.

Models the memento-mcp v1 wedge in idiomatic Jac — six closed edge types
over `Memory` nodes, plus walker analogues of `subgraph` (BFS expansion to
configurable depth), `shortestPath` (BFS shortest path with hop cap), and
`CascadeInvalidator` (reachability-based invalidation). Walkers carry an
explicit visited set because Jac 0.36 has no built-in once-per-node
visitation, which is the cycle policy this issue requires.
"""

node Memory {
    has memory_id: str = "";
    has title: str = "";
    has available: bool = True;
}

edge RelatesTo { has weight: float = 1.0; }
edge Supersedes { has weight: float = 1.0; }
edge CausedBy { has weight: float = 1.0; }
edge MitigatedBy { has weight: float = 1.0; }
edge References { has weight: float = 1.0; }
edge Implements { has weight: float = 1.0; }

walker SubgraphExplorer {
    has depth: int = 2;
    has report_nodes: list = [];
    has visited: dict = {};

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can expand with Memory entry {
        already = False;
        for mid in self.visited {
            if mid == here.memory_id {
                already = True;
            }
        }
        if not already {
            self.visited[here.memory_id] = True;
            self.report_nodes.append(here);
        }
        if self.depth > 0 {
            self.depth -= 1;
            visit [->:RelatesTo:->];
            visit [->:Supersedes:->];
            visit [->:CausedBy:->];
            visit [->:MitigatedBy:->];
            visit [->:References:->];
            visit [->:Implements:->];
        }
    }
}

walker PathFinder {
    has target_id: str = "";
    has max_hops: int = 4;
    has found_path: list = [];
    has visited: dict = {};

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can search with Memory entry {
        already = False;
        for mid in self.visited {
            if mid == here.memory_id {
                already = True;
            }
        }
        if already {
            disengage;
        }
        self.visited[here.memory_id] = True;
        if here.memory_id == self.target_id {
            self.found_path = [here];
            disengage;
        }
        if self.max_hops <= 0 {
            disengage;
        }
        self.max_hops -= 1;
        visit [->:RelatesTo:->];
        visit [->:Supersedes:->];
        visit [->:CausedBy:->];
        visit [->:MitigatedBy:->];
        visit [->:References:->];
        visit [->:Implements:->];
    }
}

walker CascadeInvalidator {
    has visited: dict = {};

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can invalidate with Memory entry {
        already = False;
        for mid in self.visited {
            if mid == here.memory_id {
                already = True;
            }
        }
        if already {
            disengage;
        }
        self.visited[here.memory_id] = True;
        here.available = False;
        print(f"Invalidated: {here.title}");
        visit [->:RelatesTo:->];
        visit [->:Supersedes:->];
        visit [->:CausedBy:->];
        visit [->:MitigatedBy:->];
        visit [->:References:->];
        visit [->:Implements:->];
    }
}

with entry {
    pitfall = Memory(memory_id="m1", title="Pitfall: off-by-one in index");
    pattern = Memory(memory_id="m2", title="Pattern: bounds-check helper");
    arch = Memory(memory_id="m3", title="Architecture: layered memory store");
    decision = Memory(memory_id="m4", title="Decision: use BFS for graph queries");

    pitfall +>:CausedBy:+> pattern;
    pattern +>:MitigatedBy:+> pitfall;
    arch +>:References:+> decision;
    decision +>:Implements:+> arch;
    pitfall +>:RelatesTo:+> arch;

    root ++> pitfall;
    root ++> pattern;
    root ++> arch;
    root ++> decision;

    print("=== Subgraph from pitfall (depth=2) ===");
    explorer = SubgraphExplorer(depth=2);
    pitfall spawn explorer;
    titles = [];
    for m in explorer.report_nodes {
        titles.append(m.title);
    }
    print(f"Nodes reached: {titles}");

    print("\n=== Shortest path pitfall -> decision (max_hops=4) ===");
    finder = PathFinder(target_id="m4", max_hops=4);
    pitfall spawn finder;
    if len(finder.found_path) > 0 {
        path_titles = [];
        for m in finder.found_path {
            path_titles.append(m.title);
        }
        print(f"Path: {path_titles}");
    } else {
        print("No path found within hop limit.");
    }

    print("\n=== Cascade invalidate from arch ===");
    arch spawn CascadeInvalidator();
}
```

