messages.content:
A maintainer on the project olosegres/jsona (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #72: Prototype pollution in Jsona.serialize() via includeNames path

`Jsona.serialize()` has a prototype pollution issue in its `includeNames` handling.

`includeNames` is used to include related resources. The README documents this as a way to include relationship names, including nested relationships with dot notation, for example:

```js
includeNames: ['categories', 'town.country']
```

However, `includeNames` is converted into a nested object tree without filtering prototype-related path segments such as `__proto__`, `constructor`, or `prototype`. As a result, a path such as `__proto__.toString` writes to `Object.prototype`.

Affected package:

```text
jsona@1.14.0
```

I also checked several versions:

```text
0.1.6  not affected
0.2.3  not affected
1.0.0  affected
1.9.7  affected
1.10.1 affected
1.13.0 affected
1.14.0 affected
```

So the likely affected range is:

```text
>= 1.0.0, <= 1.14.0
```

Relevant source:

```ts
// src/builders/ModelsSerializer.ts
setIncludeNames(includeNames) {
  if (Array.isArray(includeNames)) {
    const includeNamesTree = {};
    includeNames.forEach((namesChain) => {
      createIncludeNamesTree(namesChain, includeNamesTree);
    });
    this.includeNamesTree = includeNamesTree;
  } else {
    this.includeNamesTree = includeNames;
  }
}
```

```ts
// src/utils.ts
export function createIncludeNamesTree(namesChain, includeTree): void {
  const namesArray = namesChain.split('.');
  const currentIncludeName = namesArray.shift();
  const chainHasMoreNames = namesArray.length;

  let subTree = null;

  if (chainHasMoreNames) {
    subTree = includeTree[currentIncludeName] || {};
    createIncludeNamesTree(namesArray.join('.'), subTree);
  }

  includeTree[currentIncludeName] = subTree;
}
```

When `currentIncludeName` is `__proto__`, this line reads `Object.prototype`:

```ts
subTree = includeTree[currentIncludeName] || {};
```

The recursive call then writes attacker-controlled properties onto `Object.prototype`.

PoC:

```js
const { Jsona } = require('jsona');

delete Object.prototype.pp;

new Jsona().serialize({
  stuff: {
    id: '1',
    type: 'article',
    title: 'Hello',
  },
  includeNames: ['__proto__.pp'],
});

console.log(Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(Object.prototype, 'pp'));
console.log(({}).pp);

delete Object.prototype.pp;
```

Observed output:

```text
true
null
```

This also allows overwriting existing inherited properties. For example:

```js
const { Jsona } = require('jsona');

const originalToString = Object.prototype.toString;

new Jsona().serialize({
  stuff: {
    id: '1',
    type: 'article',
  },
  includeNames: ['__proto__.toString'],
});

console.log(Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(Object.prototype, 'toString'));
console.log(Object.prototype.toString);

try {
  console.log(String({}));
} catch (error) {
  console.log(error.name + ': ' + error.message);
}

Object.prototype.toString = originalToString;
```

Observed output:

```text
true
null
TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value
```

Expected behavior:

`includeNames` should only describe JSON:API relationship paths. Prototype-related path segments should not modify JavaScript built-in prototypes.

Actual behavior:

`includeNames` can write properties to `Object.prototype`, causing process-wide prototype pollution.

Impact:

If an application passes attacker-controlled or less-trusted relationship include paths to `Jsona.serialize()`, an attacker can pollute `Object.prototype`. This can affect unrelated objects in the same process. At minimum, this can cause denial of service by overwriting properties such as `toString`; depending on application-specific gadgets, it may also affect authorization or other business logic that reads inherited properties.

Suggested fix:

- Reject path segments equal to `__proto__`, `constructor`, or `prototype`.
- Build `includeNamesTree` using null-prototype objects, for example `Object.create(null)`.
- Use own-property checks when reading existing include tree nodes, for example `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(includeTree, currentIncludeName)`.


Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""IncludeNames tree builder: models JSON:API include path resolution and
the prototype-pollution guard that fixes it.

Entities are nodes (Resources and the IncludePath chain), the path segments
are walked by a typed-edge walker, and unsafe segments (__proto__,
constructor, prototype) are rejected at the traversal boundary. A second
walker compares the unsafe (vulnerable) and safe (patched) behavior.
"""

node Resource {
    has name: str = "";
    has kind: str = "";
    has attrs: dict = {};
}

node IncludeNode {
    has segment: str = "";
    has safe: bool = True;
    has depth: int = 0;
}

# Typed edge: a step inside an include-path chain (segment -> next).
edge SegmentStep { }

# Typed edge: a resource reachable through an include-name.
edge Includes { }

walker PathBuilder {
    has max_depth: int = 8;
    has visited: list = [];
    has blocked: list = [];
    has unsafe_segments: list = ["__proto__", "constructor", "prototype"];

    can start with Root entry {
        print(f"PathBuilder starting (max_depth={self.max_depth})");
        visit [->:SegmentStep:->];
    }

    can step with IncludeNode entry {
        seg = here.segment;
        depth = here.depth;
        here.safe = not (seg in self.unsafe_segments);
        if not here.safe {
            self.blocked.append(seg);
            print(f"  [BLOCKED] segment={seg} depth={depth}");
        } else {
            label = "safe" if here.safe else "unsafe";
            print(f"  [OK] segment={seg} depth={depth} ({label})");
        }
        if depth >= self.max_depth {
            print(f"  [STOP] max_depth reached at segment={seg}");
            disengage;
        }
        visit [->:SegmentStep:->];
    }
}

walker PollutionProbe {
    can start with Root entry {
        print("PollutionProbe: scanning reachable IncludeNodes");
        visit [->:SegmentStep:->];
    }

    can scan with IncludeNode entry {
        seg = here.segment;
        verdict = "REJECTED" if not here.safe else "ACCEPTED";
        print(f"  segment={seg} verdict={verdict} depth={here.depth}");
    }
}

with entry {
    # Build a small include-path chain that contains a dangerous segment.
    n0 = IncludeNode(segment="categories", depth=0);
    n1 = IncludeNode(segment="town", depth=1);
    n2 = IncludeNode(segment="country", depth=2);
    n3 = IncludeNode(segment="__proto__", depth=3);
    n4 = IncludeNode(segment="pp", depth=4);
    n5 = IncludeNode(segment="constructor", depth=5);
    n6 = IncludeNode(segment="prototype", depth=6);

    root +>:SegmentStep:+> n0;
    n0 +>:SegmentStep:+> n1;
    n1 +>:SegmentStep:+> n2;
    n2 +>:SegmentStep:+> n3;
    n3 +>:SegmentStep:+> n4;
    n4 +>:SegmentStep:+> n5;
    n5 +>:SegmentStep:+> n6;

    # A couple of resources reachable through the safe prefix.
    article = Resource(name="stuff", kind="article", attrs={"id": "1", "title": "Hello"});
    categories = Resource(name="categories", kind="relation", attrs={});
    town = Resource(name="town", kind="relation", attrs={});

    root +>:Includes:+> article;
    article +>:Includes:+> categories;
    categories +>:Includes:+> town;

    print("== Building include-name tree (safe mode) ==");
    builder = PathBuilder(max_depth=6);
    root spawn builder;

    print(f"\nblocked segments: {builder.blocked}");
    print(f"all segments accepted? {len(builder.blocked) == 0}");

    print("\n== PollutionProbe: re-walk to confirm rejection ==");
    probe = PollutionProbe();
    root spawn probe;
}
```

jac_tests:
test "blocks prototype-related include segments" {
    categories = IncludeNode(segment="categories", depth=0);
    proto = IncludeNode(segment="__proto__", depth=1);
    town = IncludeNode(segment="town", depth=0);
    ctor = IncludeNode(segment="constructor", depth=1);
    country = IncludeNode(segment="country", depth=0);
    prototype = IncludeNode(segment="prototype", depth=1);

    root +>:SegmentStep:+> categories;
    categories +>:SegmentStep:+> proto;
    root +>:SegmentStep:+> town;
    town +>:SegmentStep:+> ctor;
    root +>:SegmentStep:+> country;
    country +>:SegmentStep:+> prototype;

    res = root spawn PathBuilder(max_depth=8);

    assert len([root ->:SegmentStep:->]) == 3, f"wrong root branches";
    assert len(res.blocked) == 3, f"blocked segments: {res.blocked}";
    assert "__proto__" in res.blocked, f"missing __proto__: {res.blocked}";
    assert "constructor" in res.blocked, f"missing constructor: {res.blocked}";
    assert "prototype" in res.blocked, f"missing prototype: {res.blocked}";
    assert categories.safe, f"safe segment was rejected";
    assert town.safe, f"safe segment was rejected";
    assert country.safe, f"safe segment was rejected";
    assert not proto.safe, f"__proto__ was accepted";
    assert not ctor.safe, f"constructor was accepted";
    assert not prototype.safe, f"prototype was accepted";
}

test "accepts safe nested paths and preserves typed relationships" {
    n0 = IncludeNode(segment="categories", depth=0);
    n1 = IncludeNode(segment="town", depth=1);
    n2 = IncludeNode(segment="country", depth=2);

    root +>:SegmentStep:+> n0;
    n0 +>:SegmentStep:+> n1;
    n1 +>:SegmentStep:+> n2;

    article = Resource(name="stuff", kind="article", attrs={"id": "1"});
    categories = Resource(name="categories", kind="relation", attrs={});
    town = Resource(name="town", kind="relation", attrs={});

    root +>:Includes:+> article;
    article +>:Includes:+> categories;
    categories +>:Includes:+> town;

    res = root spawn PathBuilder(max_depth=8);

    assert len(res.blocked) == 0, f"safe path was blocked: {res.blocked}";
    assert n0.safe, f"categories was not accepted";
    assert n1.safe, f"town was not accepted";
    assert n2.safe, f"country was not accepted";
    assert n1 in [n0 ->:SegmentStep:->], f"town edge has wrong direction";
    assert n2 in [n1 ->:SegmentStep:->], f"country edge has wrong direction";
    assert article in [root ->:Includes:->], f"article relationship missing";
    assert categories in [article ->:Includes:->], f"categories relationship missing";
    assert town in [categories ->:Includes:->], f"town relationship missing";
}

test "stops traversal at the configured depth" {
    n0 = IncludeNode(segment="categories", depth=0);
    n1 = IncludeNode(segment="__proto__", depth=1);
    n2 = IncludeNode(segment="pp", depth=2);
    n3 = IncludeNode(segment="constructor", depth=3);

    root +>:SegmentStep:+> n0;
    n0 +>:SegmentStep:+> n1;
    n1 +>:SegmentStep:+> n2;
    n2 +>:SegmentStep:+> n3;

    res = root spawn PathBuilder(max_depth=1);

    assert len(res.blocked) == 1, f"unexpected blocked segments: {res.blocked}";
    assert "__proto__" in res.blocked, f"boundary segment was not blocked";
    assert n1 in [n0 ->:SegmentStep:->], f"expected nested segment edge";
    assert n2.safe, f"node beyond depth boundary was unexpectedly changed";
    assert n3.safe, f"node beyond depth boundary was unexpectedly changed";
}

