A maintainer on the project KevinRabbe/underworld (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #586: FIRST-PLAYABLE-001 — Overworld Biome 1 + Hell Entrance complete playable target

ID: FIRST-PLAYABLE-001

Status: **PLANNING AUTHORITY — PM2 FIRST-PLAYABLE PROGRAM / IMPLEMENTATION WIP=0**
Priority: P0 product target after current M3 closeout; does **not** alter PM1/#33 active review or landing order
Category: Product Milestone / Integrated Playable / Scope Authority
Current audited main at creation: `5def7e23de4c0947907ee4a7691956f0e6588ef3`
Existing architecture/roadmap references: #405, #369, current two-domain runtime/SAVE/gateway graph, accepted gameplay foundations
Older first-Overworld planning: #470/#499 — reusable evidence/child architecture, but product scope in those cards is superseded where it conflicts with this target once this program is promoted

## Mission

Deliver the first version of Underworld that proves the intended game rather than only a technical route:

> **one complete Overworld biome + one complete Underworld biome (Hell Entrance), with a full survival/combat/building/progression loop and production-scale foundations.**

Guiding rule:

> **Small content scope. Complete gameplay loop. Production-scale foundations.**

This issue is a product/integration authority, not a mega-source task. Existing narrow owners remain the source authorities for their systems. Missing capabilities receive bounded child cards only after owner/deduplication audit.

---

# 1. First-playable progression spine

```text
NEW CHARACTER
-> OVERWORLD BIOME 1
-> gather / hunt / explore
-> primitive Wood + Stone + Fiber survival/crafting
-> Workbench + first home
-> Axe / Pickaxe / Hoe / Build Tool
-> distinct weapon families + use-based skills
-> Rootwretch ecosystem
-> Hardened Root
-> Armor Set 1
-> ROOTWRETCH CHAMPION
   Axe-favored, roughly 3-7 minute valid matchup envelope
-> permanently enable first Underworld access
-> HELL ENTRANCE / UNDERWORLD LAYER 1
-> aggressive hostile ecosystem
-> explore / fight / build persistent fortified outpost
-> Infernal organic armor material
-> Armor Set 2
-> deeper Layer-1 expeditions
-> HELL WARDEN CLIMAX
   War-Spear-favored, roughly 3-7 minute valid matchup envelope
-> FIRST PLAYABLE COMPLETE
```

The Hell climax is the content endpoint for this milestone. Underworld Layer 2+ and Overworld Biome 2+ are later development.

---

# 2. Hard scope boundary

## In scope
- one procedural Overworld Biome 1;
- one separate procedural 3D Underworld domain: Hell Entrance;
- deterministic semantic gateway between them;
- full current survival/combat/equipment progression through both spaces;
- one Overworld boss and one Hell climax boss;
- player Building + terrain modification + persistent Underworld outposts;
- Save/Continue across the complete route;
- first-playable performance/scale acceptance.

## Explicitly out of scope
- Overworld Biome 2+;
- Underworld Layer 2+;
- metal/smelting/forge progression;
- magic;
- full stealth gameplay;
- deepest-region/endgame content;
- 80+ skill-level content tuning;
- broad armor/crafting-station catalogs;
- dedicated future weapon variants such as a true 2H Axe family;
- large future accessory/equipment-slot taxonomy.

Future concepts may influence extensible representation only when avoiding an architectural dead end. They do not authorize current content work.

---

# 3. Day-one scale rule

Some systems may evolve through playtesting. Scale-critical foundations may not rely on a knowingly disposable architecture.

Core rule:

> **If future scale changes only data/tuning, iterate freely. If it changes ownership, identity, persistence, dependency propagation, authority, spatial indexing, or asymptotic complexity, design the production-scale contract first.**

Must be scalable from day one where applicable:
- Building representation/snapping;
- structural support propagation;
- terrain mutation + terrain/Building contact indexing;
- persistence/stable semantic identity;
- world-generation determinism;
- streaming/runtime lifetime;
- AI/navigation invalidation;
- damage/destruction propagation;
- storage/resource lookup;
- content/data-definition authority;
- player ownership/permission/transaction semantics needed for later networking.

No implementation may rely on players keeping bases/world history small.

Full networked multiplayer delivery is not automatically pulled into this milestone merely for future safety; player-owned state and mutation contracts must nevertheless avoid a representation that would require architectural replacement when networking is introduced.

---

# 4. Building requirement

First playable must prove both fast modular construction and arbitrary structural geometry.

Biome-1 construction content is **Wood + Thatch only**. Stone is a terrain-engineering resource, not a stone-building tier.

Normal Mode:
- relative authored snap points;
- 2 m primary module / 1 m detail unit;
- 22.5-degree normal yaw baseline;
- deterministic target/candidate selection;
- complete starter-house construction without Advanced Mode.

Advanced Mode:
- immediate mode toggle;
- point A -> point B structural member placement;
- exact/free endpoints;
- practical unrestricted 3D orientation;
- canonical structural nodes/connections;
- same durable BuildInstance/support/persistence authority as Normal Mode.

Hard geometry acceptance:
> Given any valid A/B/C, create exact A->B, B->C, C->A triangles horizontally, vertically and in arbitrary 3D without a triangle prefab.

Biome-1 authored catalogue target is the currently locked 30-piece Wood/Thatch kit. Advanced geometry replaces authored brace/truss proliferation.

---

# 5. Structural performance requirement

Whole-connected-structure recalculation is prohibited.

Preferred contract:
- one sparse support graph;
- terrain/valid world contacts are support roots;
- fixed spatial indexing only for bounded discovery/streaming, never correctness boundaries;
- positive-cost/non-amplifying anchor-rooted support metric;
- cached best support/predecessor/source plus bounded alternate/dependency information;
- incremental dirty-front propagation;
- add/improv

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Incremental support propagation over a typed building graph with cycle detection."""

node SupportNode {
    has title: str = "";
    has year: int = 0;
    has available: bool = True;
    has walk_token: int = 0;
    has supported: bool = False;
    has dirty: bool = True;
}

node TerrainCell(SupportNode) { }

node BuildPiece(SupportNode) { }

edge Contains { }

edge Supports {
    has cost: int = 1;
}

walker SupportPropagator {
    has token: int = 1;
    has cycle_found: bool = False;
    has cycle_count: int = 0;

    can start with Root entry {
        print("support propagation started");
        visit [->:Contains:->] else {
            disengage;
        }
    }

    can propagate with TerrainCell | BuildPiece entry {
        if here.walk_token == self.token {
            self.cycle_found = True;
            self.cycle_count = self.cycle_count + 1;
            print(f"cycle detected at {here.title}");
            disengage;
        } else {
            here.walk_token = self.token;
            here.supported = True;
            here.dirty = False;
            print(f"supported: {here.title}");
            visit [->:Supports:->] else {
                disengage;
            }
        }
    }
}

with entry {
    ground = TerrainCell(title="Bedrock Contact");
    foundation = BuildPiece(title="Foundation");
    beam = BuildPiece(title="Support Beam");
    brace = BuildPiece(title="Diagonal Brace");

    root +>:Contains:+> ground;
    ground +>:Supports(cost=1):+> foundation;
    foundation +>:Supports(cost=2):+> beam;
    beam +>:Supports(cost=1):+> brace;
    brace +>:Supports(cost=1):+> beam;

    root spawn SupportPropagator();
}
```

