A maintainer on the project patrickkelley/gosuspoon (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #30: GosuEnvironment.scanTypeNames lacks symlink cycle detection leading to StackOverflowError

## Summary
`GosuEnvironment.scanTypeNames` recursively calls `collect(File root, File dir, List<String> out)`. For each entry in `dir.listFiles()`, it checks `f.isDirectory()` and unconditionally recurses into `collect(root, f, out)`. Because `File.isDirectory()` resolves and follows symbolic links, any symlink pointing to an ancestor or enclosing directory creates an infinite directory recursion, resulting in a `StackOverflowError`.

## Location
- `spoon-gosu/src/main/java/spoon/gosu/GosuEnvironment.java:114-126`
```java
private static void collect(File root, File dir, List<String> out) {
    File[] files = dir.listFiles();
    if (files == null) {
        return;
    }
    for (File f : files) {
        if (f.isDirectory()) {
            collect(root, f, out);
        } else if (f.getName().endsWith(".gs") || f.getName().endsWith(".gsx")) {
            out.add(fullyQualifiedName(root, f));
        }
    }
}
```

## Reproduction
```bash
mkdir -p /tmp/cycle-demo/sub
ln -s /tmp/cycle-demo /tmp/cycle-demo/sub/loop
```
Calling `GosuEnvironment.initialize(List.of(new File("/tmp/cycle-demo"))).scanTypeNames(...)` enters unbounded recursion across `/tmp/cycle-demo/sub/loop/sub/loop/...` until `java.lang.StackOverflowError`.

## Impact
Projects containing circular directory symlinks, recursive build directories, or IDE link structures crash Spoon during startup before parsing begins.

## Suggested fix
Track canonical or real paths using a visited `Set<Path>` or use `Files.walkFileTree` with a loop/cycle detector:
```java
Path realPath = f.toPath().toRealPath();
if (visited.add(realPath)) {
    collect(root, f, out, visited);
}
```

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Symlink-safe directory scanning with explicit canonical-path cycle detection."""

node Directory {
    has title: str = "";
    has year: int = 0;
    has available: bool = True;
    has canonical_path: str = "";
    has is_link: bool = False;
}

node SourceFile {
    has title: str = "";
    has year: int = 0;
    has available: bool = True;
    has extension: str = "";
}

edge Contains {
    has kind: str = "";
}

walker TypeScanner {
    has visited_paths: list[str] = [];
    has discovered_files: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [->:Contains:->] else {
            disengage;
        }
    }

    can scan with Directory entry {
        if here.canonical_path in self.visited_paths {
            print(f"[cycle] skipped {here.title} at {here.canonical_path}");
        } else {
            self.visited_paths.append(here.canonical_path);
            print(f"[directory] scanning {here.title} at {here.canonical_path}");
            visit [->:Contains:->] else {
                disengage;
            }
        }
    }

    can collect_file with SourceFile entry {
        self.discovered_files.append(here.title);
        print(f"[file] discovered {here.title}{here.extension}");
    }
}

with entry {
    workspace = Directory(
        title="cycle-demo",
        canonical_path="/tmp/cycle-demo"
    );
    subdir = Directory(
        title="sub",
        canonical_path="/tmp/cycle-demo/sub"
    );
    loop = Directory(
        title="loop",
        canonical_path="/tmp/cycle-demo",
        is_link=True
    );
    main_file = SourceFile(
        title="Main",
        extension=".gs"
    );
    nested_file = SourceFile(
        title="Nested",
        extension=".gsx"
    );

    root +>:Contains(kind="scan root"):+> workspace;
    workspace +>:Contains(kind="source file"):+> main_file;
